{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789336171,"nonce":"V6VNJ86Az7m+pfXIfGn9rrnY7jg=","title":"A block nothing reaches joins the natural loop it branches into","message":"`LoopForest::detect` collects a loop's body by flooding predecessors\nback from each latch. A block const_fold has cut off (a folded branch\nleaves its dead arm in the function, still ending in a branch to the\nloop's join block) is a predecessor of that join and so lands in the\nbody, though the header does not dominate it and nothing reaches it.\n\nSeen on `bench_closure_call.py`: the dead ZeroDivisionError arm of a\n`% 8` carries calls and a store to the pending-exception global, so LICM\nsaw an impure call in the loop and hoisted nothing out of it, including\nthe box reads the loop re-did every iteration.\n\nFix: a predecessor the header does not dominate is not part of the loop.\nRemoving unreachable blocks after folding would also settle it, and is\nstill owed for code size.","files":null}]}